export const body = "<p>Chapter 05: Level Pipeline</p>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n\n<p>As said in Chapter 2, we will focus on first- and third-person cameras. That means we will compose dynamic 3D spaces instead of static 2D images. If it helps, you can think of 3D composition in terms of interior design, instead of painting.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/83af14e1d92d542fdb02.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/83af14e1d92d542fdb02.webp\" width=\"710\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:398px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Pre-production</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>This stage is all about preparation. On the art side, the top priority of this stage is to set a unified vision for the game, as well as defining the technical specifications.</p>\n\n<p>As a level artist, your primary goal during this period will be <strong>understanding the art direction</strong>, <strong>the gameplay</strong>, and <strong>the technical constraints</strong>. Having a firm grasp on these elements early on will dispel potential creative confusion down the line.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bf9d278705acbceece0f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bf9d278705acbceece0f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"187\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Game Design Document</h3>\n\n<p>A game design document (GDD) is an all-encompassing guideline that unifies the dev team under one consistent vision. This is a living document, so it will change over the course of production.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4b3589f2735849463f23.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4b3589f2735849463f23.webp\" width=\"955\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Art bible</h3>\n\n<p>Look Development (lookdev for short) is about defining the art direction of the game. It involves creating the materials, textures, and geometric forms that drive the look of the environments and props.</p>\n\n<p>The outcome of this process is an Art Bible (sometimes called a Style Guide). It is a document detailing how to achieve the intended look for the game.</p>\n\n<p>For an art style overview, check out this article on realistic versus stylized.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2ee1a19f55d8681ef66c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2ee1a19f55d8681ef66c.webp\" width=\"798\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:477px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Vertical slice</h3>\n\n<p>A vertical slice is a polished, early build of the game consisting of its major systems and key features. This is:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Not a full game</li><li>Player-experience focused</li><li>Representative of the final quality</li></ul>\n\n<p>Sometimes, it is also called “First-playable,” “Proof of Concept,” or “Minimum Viable Product.”</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/45af169c50656340426c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/45af169c50656340426c.webp\" width=\"792\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:487px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Beautiful corner</h3>\n\n<p>For full-level projects, we will have to submit some “beautiful corner” screenshots. The purpose is to showcase our artistic and technical capacity to the client so that they can decide whether or not we can create full levels.</p>\n\n<p>For level production, the beautiful corner sets the aesthetic requirements, direction and the quality bar to move forward with the level. Besides, it also establishes estimates and scope for the level.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bac226fc3cab539b0565.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bac226fc3cab539b0565.webp\" width=\"922\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:551px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Production</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>With the foundation finished, budget approved and schedule set, production will commence. This phase is essentially building the game based on the GDD and the Art Bible.</p>\n\n<p>Below are 3 significant milestones during this phase.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/75e249838a952d18b161.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/75e249838a952d18b161.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"222\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Pre-alpha</h3>\n\n<p>Pre-alpha is the time when the majority of the content is developed. Big decisions need to be made to lock the features and amount of content. Existing content may get cut, or new content may be added to improve gameplay.</p>\n\n<p>As a level artist, this is the period for asset creation. In level art production, there are 4 broad categories of assets:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Hero assets</strong></li><li><strong>Props</strong></li><li><strong>Modular assets</strong></li><li><strong>Vista assets</strong></li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5b3dafbaf33476f57925.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5b3dafbaf33476f57925.webp\" width=\"884\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:527px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Hero assets</h3>\n\n<p>As the name suggest, hero assets are the most important assets in a level. They have unique designs and gameplay inplications. Because of that, they often have very detailed concept arts, and require collaboration with other departments.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/58cac90d02dadd120837.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/58cac90d02dadd120837.webp\" width=\"1000\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Props</h3>\n\n<p>A good definition for a prop is anything that supports the scene, but is not part of the level layout. There are two general types of props:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Static props </strong>are purely made to populate the environment</li><li><strong>Dynamic props </strong>are interactable objects in the environment</li></ul>\n\n<p>For example, the urns you can break in <em>Demon’s Souls</em> are dynamic props.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c5feececc4b222b3bdb8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c5feececc4b222b3bdb8.webp\" width=\"876\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:524px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Modular assets</h3>\n\n<p>Modular assets are responsible for the majority of level creation. They are reusable pieces that can be mixed and matched in various ways to quickly populate a vast environment.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c7e3a3afe4f8fb963355.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c7e3a3afe4f8fb963355.webp\" width=\"901\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:538px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Modular assets</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>Modules are particularly useful for architecture. Notre Dame only consists of 10 different repeated pieces when you break it down.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d9b59ca34449702fcdbe.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d9b59ca34449702fcdbe.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"301\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>But be careful when using modules for organic objects. Here, different types of rock are mixed together, which is unrealistic.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/16bb3ab1fc21c6897567.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/16bb3ab1fc21c6897567.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"353\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Vista assets</h3>\n\n<p>Vistas are literal backgrounds in level creation. They are often presented from a distance to build an atmosphere for the level. Therefore, they have lower polycounts compared to the previous types.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1c7de5b662608c06066a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1c7de5b662608c06066a.webp\" width=\"927\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:554px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXTRA –</strong> <strong>Landscape sculpting</strong></p>\n\n<p>Level artists and designers also work on the terrain of a level. The workflow often consists of 4 steps:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Terrain layout</strong>: draw a simple diagram of the landscape with labels</li><li><strong>Blockout</strong>: sculpt rough shapes for the landscape, with attention to flow and scale</li><li><strong>Metrics</strong>: measure travel times, heights, and traversable ramps/slopes</li><li><strong>Art pass</strong>: sculpt and paint smaller details, apply set dressing</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b28e5be57fbf280d45bd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b28e5be57fbf280d45bd.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"550\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a81a72a36517e2938c13.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a81a72a36517e2938c13.webp\" width=\"640\" height=\"351\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Alpha</h3>\n\n<p>Once the game hits Alpha, it is playable from start to finish with all main features. A few things, such as art assets still need to be added, but gameplay functionality should work properly.</p>\n\n<p>At the end of Alpha stage, is most commonly considered content complete. The expectation is that no new content is required from this point on.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/18ed015a5a93a07f915d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/18ed015a5a93a07f915d.webp\" width=\"888\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:531px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Beta</h3>\n\n<p>Beta is when all the content and assets are integrated. The focus is now on optimization rather than adding new functions or features.</p>\n\n<p>The end of Beta is a shippable product, meaning the end user will see what we have generated. Fixing all priority and critical bugs is essential. The goal is to have no bugs left.</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes, the beta build is open for public access, also known as beta testing. This is usually done for game with an online multiplayer mode.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/07d0be31c0b7a0c032ce.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/07d0be31c0b7a0c032ce.webp\" width=\"882\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:527px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>EXTRA – Goldmaster</h3>\n\n<p>Goldmaster is when the game is final and ready to be sent to publishing outlets and released to the public.</p>\n\n<p>If the game is released physically, the data will be burnt into a gold disc. Hence the term “gold master” or “going gold.” But as you can see in the opposite picture, the disc doesn’t have to be gold.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7730136fc24ba0eb5bcc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7730136fc24ba0eb5bcc.webp\" width=\"617\" height=\"565\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Post-production</h2>\n\n<p>After the game is shipped, it could still receive further development in the form of patches or downloadable content (DLC).</p>\n\n<p>Patches are meant to fix performance issues and balance the gameplay. DLCs, on the other hand, are intended to add new content to the game.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b58410192c200a3f4f8a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b58410192c200a3f4f8a.webp\" width=\"811\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:485px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Level making process overview</h2>\n\n<p>To wrap up this chapter, we will have an overview of the level-making process from a level artist’s perspective. Step by step:</p>\n\n<ol><li><strong>Reference gathering</strong></li><li><strong>Blockout</strong></li><li><strong>Modelling</strong></li><li><strong>Composition</strong></li><li><strong>Polishing</strong></li></ol>\n\n<p>Keep in mind that everything is subject to iteration throughout the process based on playtest feedback.</p>\n\n<p>For this section, we use this article as an example. This is just a frame of reference, so it doesn’t precisely reflect real production (where things are more unpredictable).</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2a929316e68ff088b3f6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2a929316e68ff088b3f6.webp\" width=\"870\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:520px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Reference gathering</strong></p>\n\n<p>Before you start building the assets or sculpting the terrain, you should have a robust mood board in place. A mood board is a collection of references based on the theme of your level. In this example, the theme is Wild West.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9c1a50b9b9c124dc9ce7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9c1a50b9b9c124dc9ce7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"342\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Blockout</strong></p>\n\n<p>As you gather references, your level designer will work on the blockout, the rough layout of your level. During this stage, the most important guidelines to follow are scale and gameplay metrics.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b988d7dc33a06226d2d5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b988d7dc33a06226d2d5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Modelling &amp; texturing</strong></p>\n\n<p>By reading the design brief, you will know what the metrics for gameplay are, and how your assets should be scaled (because the scale won’t be 1:1 to real life no matter the art style). With the metrics and scale in mind, you can begin creating proxy models to place into the blockout. Some basic coloring for your proxy models can help enhance clarity for playtesting.</p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a73d03108a6630ac28db.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a73d03108a6630ac28db.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Composition</strong></p>\n\n<p>After the main assets are made, it’s time to think about composition. Other than to make an eye-pleasing image, composition is also about showing the direction/objective of your level. By reconciling these two goals, your level will look good and play well.</p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1e9d3d595c66350ed90f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1e9d3d595c66350ed90f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Iterating based on feedback</strong></p>\n\n<p>After the main assets are made, it’s time to think about composition. Other than to make an eye-pleasing image, composition is also about showing the direction/objective of your level. By reconciling these two goals, your level will look good and play well.Your level should be routinely playtested by yourself, your designer, and QA specialists throughout the development cycle. The playtest sessions will provide you with feedback—art-wise, design-wise—so that you and your designer can iterate on the level. In these pictures, you can see how different the composition has become.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a05c6da353ac603e0cd1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a05c6da353ac603e0cd1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"353\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Polishing</strong></p>\n\n<p>Once your level has met the requirements for both gameplay and composition, it’s time for detailed dressing (adding story to the environment), texturing your proxy models, and adding basic lighting.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/785f730cdd2336116456.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/785f730cdd2336116456.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 27\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/07d0be31c0b7a0c032ce.webp", "assets/library-inline/16bb3ab1fc21c6897567.webp", "assets/library-inline/18ed015a5a93a07f915d.webp", "assets/library-inline/1c7de5b662608c06066a.webp", "assets/library-inline/1e9d3d595c66350ed90f.webp", "assets/library-inline/2a929316e68ff088b3f6.webp", "assets/library-inline/2ee1a19f55d8681ef66c.webp", "assets/library-inline/45af169c50656340426c.webp", "assets/library-inline/4b3589f2735849463f23.webp", "assets/library-inline/58cac90d02dadd120837.webp", "assets/library-inline/5b3dafbaf33476f57925.webp", "assets/library-inline/75e249838a952d18b161.webp", "assets/library-inline/7730136fc24ba0eb5bcc.webp", "assets/library-inline/785f730cdd2336116456.webp", "assets/library-inline/83af14e1d92d542fdb02.webp", "assets/library-inline/9c1a50b9b9c124dc9ce7.webp", "assets/library-inline/a05c6da353ac603e0cd1.webp", "assets/library-inline/a73d03108a6630ac28db.webp", "assets/library-inline/a81a72a36517e2938c13.webp", "assets/library-inline/b28e5be57fbf280d45bd.webp", "assets/library-inline/b58410192c200a3f4f8a.webp", "assets/library-inline/b988d7dc33a06226d2d5.webp", "assets/library-inline/bac226fc3cab539b0565.webp", "assets/library-inline/bf9d278705acbceece0f.webp", "assets/library-inline/c5feececc4b222b3bdb8.webp", "assets/library-inline/c7e3a3afe4f8fb963355.webp", "assets/library-inline/d9b59ca34449702fcdbe.webp"];
